From 5c82866644343fc591e7af672ac1e7e8a9929905 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: manerger <filidog82@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 17:52:29 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 15/21] mb/asustek_computer/k53sc: Enable suspend to RAM

The board did not support S3 at all. Without HAVE_ACPI_RESUME,
sleepstates.asl leaves SSFG at 0x09 so _S3 is never declared, and the OS
reported only "(supports S0 S1 S4 S5)". Suspending gave S1 or a software
freeze, never suspend to RAM.

Select HAVE_ACPI_RESUME, as the other Sandy Bridge laptops with this
southbridge do. Native raminit already implements the resume path.

Tested on an ASUS K53SC with an i7-3840QM: the OS now reports
"(supports S0 S1 S3 S4 S5)", and a suspend/resume cycle completes with
the session intact:

  PM: suspend entry (deep)
  ACPI: PM: Preparing to enter system sleep state S3
  ACPI: PM: Restoring platform NVS memory
  PM: suspend exit

Note the fan runs at full speed while suspended and stays there after
resume. That is the EC failing to track the host power state, the same
behaviour seen when the board refuses to drop its rails at S5, and it is
not caused by this change - the fan is under EC control and the host is
not running while suspended. Stock's OemSxSmm S3 handlers only drive
GPIOs for the suspend LED (GPO_BLINK and GP_LVL), so they do not address
it either.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/mainboard/asustek_computer/k53sc/Kconfig | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/src/mainboard/asustek_computer/k53sc/Kconfig b/src/mainboard/asustek_computer/k53sc/Kconfig
index f321daf770..9dd1424c64 100644
--- a/src/mainboard/asustek_computer/k53sc/Kconfig
+++ b/src/mainboard/asustek_computer/k53sc/Kconfig
@@ -7,6 +7,7 @@ config BOARD_SPECIFIC_OPTIONS
 	select AZALIA_USE_LEGACY_VERB_TABLE
 	select BOARD_ROMSIZE_KB_4096
 	select EC_ACPI
+	select HAVE_ACPI_RESUME
 	select HAVE_ACPI_TABLES
 	select INTEL_GMA_HAVE_VBT
 	select INTEL_INT15
